WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.202
Installation describes the possibility that adversary-controlled software, access,

00:00:03.202 --> 00:00:05.916
or configuration becomes established after initial activity.

00:00:05.916 --> 00:00:09.018
Persistence risk should be handled carefully and defensively.

00:00:09.018 --> 00:00:14.446
Responders do not need a catalog of persistence techniques to use this stage well;

00:00:14.446 --> 00:00:18.711
they need to know what changed, whether the change is authorized,

00:00:18.711 --> 00:00:21.425
and whether it could allow continued access.

00:00:21.425 --> 00:00:25.302
Endpoint monitoring, application control, change detection, startup and service review,

00:00:25.302 --> 00:00:29.179
integrity monitoring, privileged access control, and configuration baselines all help.

00:00:29.179 --> 00:00:31.118
This stage also requires humility.

00:00:31.118 --> 00:00:34.607
Many modern incidents do not involve traditional malware installation.

00:00:34.607 --> 00:00:39.260
Cloud, SaaS, and identity-driven incidents may leave evidence in tokens, sessions, roles,

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:41.586
automation rules, OAuth grants, API keys,

00:00:41.586 --> 00:00:45.076
or configuration changes rather than on a single endpoint.

00:00:45.076 --> 00:00:48.953
Analysts should avoid forcing the evidence into a malware-centered story.

00:00:48.953 --> 00:00:54.381
Instead, ask what capability or access could persist, where it would appear in telemetry,

00:00:54.381 --> 00:00:59.809
who owns the affected system, and what containment would reduce risk while preserving evidence.

00:00:59.809 --> 00:01:06.013
Good use of this stage helps teams find unauthorized change and decide whether recovery requires cleanup,

00:01:06.013 --> 00:01:07.951
rebuild, revocation, or configuration correction.

00:01:07.951 --> 00:01:11.828
Defenders should also consider how persistence risk changes by platform.

00:01:11.828 --> 00:01:16.093
Endpoint, server, cloud, SaaS, and identity systems all leave different evidence.

00:01:16.093 --> 00:01:20.746
A strong review looks across those layers instead of assuming that one

00:01:20.746 --> 00:01:23.460
endpoint scan can answer every persistence question.
